1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,630
This is ApexMarket Mission Control: one place to route batch inference, inspect verification, and manage exact settlement.

2
00:00:06,630 --> 00:00:09,750
This walkthrough uses the explicitly labeled simulation mode.

3
00:00:09,750 --> 00:00:15,990
The dashboard also connects to the FastAPI engine through live WebSocket telemetry and server-side API routes.

4
00:00:15,990 --> 00:00:19,500
The dark, translucent interface keeps operational decisions in focus.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,611
Every new session starts with the confidential air-gap switched on.

6
00:00:23,611 --> 00:00:27,583
New workloads are classified confidential and stay on the trusted anchor.

7
00:00:27,583 --> 00:00:30,472
Spot providers are visibly isolated in the topology.

8
00:00:30,472 --> 00:00:34,444
This control sets the sensitivity sent with the actual batch request.

9
00:00:34,444 --> 00:00:39,500
A running batch keeps its original classification, so its privacy policy cannot change midway.

10
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,609
Batch submission accepts prompt templates, a prompt count, and a maximum spending budget.

11
00:00:44,609 --> 00:00:49,573
The interface validates the request before submission, while the engine enforces its own limits.

12
00:00:49,573 --> 00:00:53,827
In this example, we switch to synthetic data and submit fifty prompts.

13
00:00:53,827 --> 00:00:57,018
Jobs show their classification, completion progress, and verified cost.

14
00:00:57,018 --> 00:00:59,500
Completed results download in original prompt order.

15
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,288
With confidential routing disabled for synthetic work, the topology connects the central router to eligible providers.

16
00:01:05,288 --> 00:01:07,602
Curved edges animate while requests are active.

17
00:01:07,602 --> 00:01:11,898
Their widths follow reported throughput, or active request counts when throughput is unavailable.

18
00:01:11,898 --> 00:01:16,856
Green identifies healthy routing, amber highlights slow responses, and red marks failed or slashed providers.

19
00:01:16,856 --> 00:01:19,500
Live telemetry reconnects automatically if the connection drops.

20
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,141
The chaos control injects degraded output into the configured local mock provider.

21
00:01:24,141 --> 00:01:30,697
In this simulated walkthrough, the affected node turns red and the verification panel illustrates the rejection and anchor fallback.

22
00:01:30,697 --> 00:01:34,493
Live mode reads stored shred attestations rather than inventing successful checks.

23
00:01:34,493 --> 00:01:40,014
The five-tier design covers local token billing, rotated canaries, anchor sampling, logprob scoring, and semantic judgment.

24
00:01:40,014 --> 00:01:41,739
Failed work earns no payout.

25
00:01:41,739 --> 00:01:44,500
Restoring the mock provider is a single action.

26
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,542
The cost chart reads the repository benchmark CSV directly and refreshes periodically.

27
00:01:48,542 --> 00:01:58,283
Across the three recorded runs, the single-provider cost is zero point zero zero one three six one dollars, and the verified router cost is zero point zero zero zero nine two three dollars.

28
00:01:58,283 --> 00:02:00,940
That is a thirty-two point one eight percent difference.

29
00:02:00,940 --> 00:02:05,958
The Batch API bar is explicitly a configurable price scenario unless measured Batch API rows are added.

30
00:02:05,958 --> 00:02:09,500
The source CSV does not record whether those runs used live credentials.

31
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:12,578
Switch to makespan to inspect the time tradeoff.

32
00:02:12,578 --> 00:02:19,667
The recorded average is about three point two nine seconds for the baseline and three point nine seconds for the verified route.

33
00:02:19,667 --> 00:02:20,956
Individual runs remain selectable.

34
00:02:20,956 --> 00:02:24,500
Asynchronous Batch API delivery is distinguished from the recorded synchronous measurements.

35
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,712
The ledger display preserves six decimal places and shows the underlying integer micro-dollar balance.

36
00:02:29,712 --> 00:02:32,067
Probation earnings stay separate from withdrawable funds.

37
00:02:32,067 --> 00:02:37,788
Each node's payable amount rounds down to whole cents, while its sub-cent remainder remains in the ledger.

38
00:02:37,788 --> 00:02:42,500
This prevents tiny batches from losing value through floating-point rounding or repeated payout fees.

39
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,797
Settle Now submits whole-cent node balances and keeps the remainder.

40
00:02:45,797 --> 00:02:49,992
Here, the simulation moves two dollars and ninety-eight cents to pending without moving external money.

41
00:02:49,992 --> 00:02:53,347
The sandbox backend uses durable reservations, deterministic batch identifiers, and idempotent reconciliation.

42
00:02:53,347 --> 00:02:56,144
Fifty backend tests passed, including payout retries and competing reservations.

43
00:02:56,144 --> 00:02:59,500
ApexMarket: route intelligently, verify every shred, and pay only for verified work.
